# An issue spec is an engineering contract

An agent picking up this issue reads **only** this file and the spec it links. Anything you leave out
becomes something an agent invents differently from the next agent.

Format: `---` frontmatter with `title` and `labels`, then the `### ` sections from
`.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/agent-task.yml`. Every section is required; Gate 0 parses both the same way.

The sections usually done badly:

- **Contract** — copy-pasteable signatures, DDL, or types. If you cannot write it, the issue is not
  ready and something it depends on has to land first.
- **Acceptance Criteria** — observable behaviours, one per line, at least two checkboxes. "Handles
  errors correctly" is not checkable; "returns null rather than throwing when the ciphertext cannot be
  decrypted" is.
- **Required Tests** — named cases, at least two, including a failure or edge case. An agent writes
  exactly the tests listed.
- **Out of Scope** — name the adjacent code that will look tempting to clean up.

Also required: explicit `CREATE`/`MODIFY`/`DELETE` paths, a performance budget or `n/a`,
`Dependencies`, and runnable verification commands.

Where two issues share a seam, **write the type once and paste the same text into both**. Do not have
the later issue guess at what the earlier one will produce.

Validate and publish:

```bash
pnpm gh:issues --dry-run   # Gate 0's own rules, before touching GitHub
pnpm gh:issues             # create or update; matched by title, so it never duplicates
```

Changing a `title` creates a second issue rather than renaming the first.
